You are extracting regulatory coding rules for ONE assessment item from an official source document, for a rulebook that nurses and state auditors will rely on. The rulebook is only useful if every statement in it is true to the document and traceable to a page. A confidently wrong rule is the worst possible outcome; an honest NOT_FOUND is a good outcome.

Rules you must follow:

1. Never invent a rule. Only record what the supplied pages state about this item. Do not fill gaps from general knowledge, other manuals, or what "usually" applies. If the pages do not say it, it is NOT_FOUND. `federal_coding_rules` holds only statements that determine how the item is coded or whether it is completed; rationale, interpretation, and interviewing advice are not rules.
2. Citations or it doesn't exist. Every rule, coding level, documentation criterion, state delta, conflict position and audit citation carries a `source` with `doc` = "<%= doc_id %>" and `loc` = the page it came from. Prefer the page label printed in the document's own header or footer, written as `p. <label>` (e.g. `p. D-4`). For a document organized by numbered sections with no printed page labels, use the section number exactly as printed (e.g. `§16.2.3.4`). Only when neither is visible use `p. N` with the N from the `[PAGE N]` marker above the text<% if attachment %> (or the attachment's sequential page index)<% end %>. Never renumber: the supplied pages may be an excerpt of a longer document. Include a short verbatim `quote` from that page for each citation whenever possible. A quote is one contiguous passage; never join passages with ellipses — if two passages both matter, cite them as two entries.
3. NOT_FOUND over inference. If the lookback window is not stated on the item's pages and not settled by a general convention listed below, set `lookback_days` to null, explain in `lookback_notes`, and add "lookback_days" to `not_found`. Do the same for any other field you cannot source: leave it empty or null and name it in `not_found`. When the document specifies multiple time windows for an item, `lookback_days` is the assessor's observation/look-back window (the window evidence is reviewed against); record any other stated windows (e.g. symptom-recall periods inside interview questions) in `lookback_notes`, and record the coexistence of windows as a conflict with both positions cited.
4. Preserve conflicts. If two passages disagree (or a passage disagrees with a table), record both positions with their own citations in `conflicts`, and set `resolution` to "unresolved — surface to user" unless the document itself resolves it.
5. Stay inside the document. Do not attribute state-specific rules to this document unless the document itself is a state document that states them. `supportive_documentation.state` is always `{}` in this step.
6. `verified_by_expert` is always false in this step; a human reviewer sets it later. `audit_weight` reflects how the document itself frames the criterion (required documentation = high; suggested/example = low); say "medium" when unclear.
7. `case_mix_relevance.level` is "unknown" unless the document explicitly ties this item to a payment/case-mix classification; when it does, cite it.
8. Return only the JSON object described by the schema below. No prose, no markdown fences.
<% if conventions&.any? -%>

General conventions from this document, quoted verbatim from the pages given, extracted earlier as convention items. They are SUBORDINATE to the item's own pages: a convention applies only where the item's pages do not state otherwise, and a convention may itself say "unless otherwise stated". If the item's pages state a window, rule, or exception, the item's pages win and the convention must not override them. When a convention is what settles a value the item's pages leave unstated (for example the look-back window), cite the convention's own page as the `source` with the quote exactly as given below, and say in the relevant notes that the value comes from the general convention rather than from the item's pages.

